'''''Senegalia nigrescens''''', the '''knobthorn''', is a deciduous African tree, growing up to 18&nbsp;m tall,<ref name=plantz>{{cite web |last=Masupa |first=Thabo |title=''Senegalia nigrescens'' (as ''Acacia nigrescens'')|url=http://www.plantzafrica.com/plantab/acacianigrescens.htm |work=PlantZAfrica.com |publisher=National Herbarium, Pretoria |accessdate=7 October 2012}}</ref> that is found in savanna regions from West Africa to South Africa. The tree is resistant to drought, not resistant to frost and its hard wood is resistant to termites.<ref name=plantz/> 

== Ecology ==
Giraffes often browse on the flowers and foliage of this tree, while the seed pods and foliage are browsed on by a range of mammals, including elephants.<ref>{{cite book | last = Van Wyk | first = Piet | title=Field guide to the TREES of the Kruger National Park|publisher=Struik | location = Cape Town | date = 2008 | page = 69 | isbn = 978-1-77007-759-1}}</ref>

It has been hypothesized that giraffes also act as pollinators. Its spicate inflorescences are too long to be protected by thorns and lack any chemical defenses, its flowers are pale rather than brightly colored as is typical of insect-pollinated species, and it blooms in the late dry season in September when other foods are less available.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=du Toit |first=J. T. |date=1992 |title=Winning by a neck. |url=https://www.naturalhistorymag.com/picks-from-the-past/12487/winning-by-a-neck |journal=Natural History |volume=8 |pages=29-32}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Dutoit |first=J.t. |date=1990 |title=Giraffe feeding on Acacia flowers: predation or pollination? |url=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1365-2028.1990.tb01137.x |journal=African Journal of Ecology |language=en |volume=28 |issue=1 |pages=63–68 |doi=10.1111/j.1365-2028.1990.tb01137.x |issn=1365-2028|url-access=subscription }}</ref> However, observed reductions in fruit set within feeding range of the giraffe suggest predation rather than pollination, with effective pollination more likely performed by insects or birds.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Fleming |first=Patricia A. |last2=Hofmeyr |first2=Sally D. |last3=Nicolson |first3=Sue W. |last4=du Toit |first4=Johan T. |date=2006 |title=Are Giraffes Pollinators or Flower Predators of Acacia nigrescens in Kruger National Park, South Africa? |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/4092044 |journal=Journal of Tropical Ecology |volume=22 |issue=3 |pages=247–253 |issn=0266-4674}}</ref>

== Uses ==
An ointment made from the roots has traditionally been used to treat convulsions.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Cumes |first=David |title=Healing trees & plants of the Lowveld |date=2009 |publisher=Struik Nature |others=Rael Loon, Dries Bester |isbn=978-1-77007-833-8 |location=Cape Town, South Africa |oclc=506229195}}</ref>

